\section{Technical Challenges}

\subsection{Path Tiles}

When the player creates a path, orange tiles appear on the grid. These tiles show the temporary path of the player.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.35\textwidth]{orange-tiles.png}
    \caption{Orange tiles created by the player path.}
    \label{fig:orange-tiles}
\end{figure}

At first, I created and destroyed these tiles every time. This put unnecessary load on the system. Creating many objects during gameplay can increase CPU usage and cause performance problems.

The solution was object pooling. Instead of destroying a tile, I keep it in a pool. When I need a new path tile, I take one from the pool. This reduces the number of new object creations during gameplay.

The profiler images show this difference. The first image shows the spikes that happen while creating a path tile. This was taken from the Profiler when I created the first path after starting the game.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{spikes.png}
    \caption{CPU spikes while creating path tiles.}
    \label{fig:spikes}
\end{figure}

The next two images compare the first run and the second run. The first one shows the first time I created the path. The second one shows when I created the same path for the second time. We can clearly see the drop in CPU usage.

