\subsection{Ball Movement}

At first, I did not use Unity ball physics. The game is grid-based, and I thought that using thousands of grid colliders would be expensive.

To handle ball movement without Unity Physics, I divided the movement into smaller steps. Otherwise, tunneling or missed collisions could happen. When a collision was detected, the system used the last safe position of the ball. Then it reflected the ball direction.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{sub-stepped.png}
    \caption{Sub-stepped probing technique for ball movement.}
    \label{fig:sub-stepped}
\end{figure}

Later, I tried Unity PhysX after Atay Hoca suggested it. I first expected it to cause performance problems, but the result was surprising. The pure physics version was faster than my custom movement system, and also made the ball collisions more realistic.

After that, I made one more improvement for the colliders. Instead of using one collider for each blocked cell, the grid uses fewer and larger colliders.
Horizontal blocked cells are merged into larger BoxColliders. This made the best performance result.

PhysX is fast in this case because it has optimized systems for collision detection and contact solving. It also uses native C++ data structures. Even though real physics sounds heavier, it can be faster than repeated collision checks written in C\#.

In the final version, the balls do not collide with thousands of small cell colliders. They collide with fewer and larger static colliders. This is a good case for PhysX.

The new BallController also does less custom work. It mostly sets and keeps the velocity, handles gameplay effects, and lets PhysX choose the bounce direction.

The next plot shows the FPS difference between the old sub-stepped solution and the latest PhysX solution.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{avg_fps_by_technique.png}
    \caption{Average FPS by ball movement technique.}
    \label{fig:avg-fps-by-technique}
\end{figure}

